Here’s the **quick and correct way to push your local shell script (or any files) from a Unix terminal to your GitHub repo** 👇

***

## ✅ **1. Go to your project folder**

```sh
cd /path/to/your/project
```

## ✅ **2. Initialize Git (only if not initialized already)**

```sh
git init
```

## ✅ **3. Add the GitHub repository as `origin`**

Copy your repo URL from GitHub (HTTPS recommended), for example:

    https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo-name>.git

Add it:

```sh
git remote add origin https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo-name>.git
```

If remote already exists and gives error:

```sh
git remote set-url origin https://github.com/<your-username>/<repo-name>.git
```

***

## ✅ **4. Add all files (including your shell script)**

```sh
git add .
```

Or add a specific script:

```sh
git add script.sh
```

***

## ✅ **5. Commit your changes**

```sh
git commit -m "Add shell script"
```

***

## ✅ **6. Push to GitHub**

For the **first push**:

```sh
git push -u origin main
```

If your repo uses `master` branch:

```sh
git push -u origin master
```

***

## 🎉 That’s it!

Your files are now uploaded to GitHub.

***

